{
    "id": "backend-apis",
    "title": "Backend Development & APIs",
    "description": "Learn how servers expose data and logic through APIs.",
    "sections": [
        {
            "id": "client-server-http",
            "title": "The Client-Server Model & HTTP",
            "pages": [
                {
                    "content": "In the client-server model, a client such as a browser or app sends an HTTP request. The server receives it, processes it, and sends back an HTTP response. The client initiates the exchange.",
                    "examples": [
                        "Client  --request-->  Server",
                        "Client <--response--  Server"
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "content": "An HTTP response provides the server's result back to the client. It includes a status code and often data, such as JSON. A response does not replace the original request.",
                    "examples": [
                        "HTTP/1.1 200 OK",
                        "Content-Type: application/json",
                        "{ \"name\": \"Ava\" }"
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "content": "HTTP methods describe the action. GET retrieves data. POST creates a new resource. PUT or PATCH updates an existing resource. DELETE removes a resource.",
                    "examples": [
                        "GET /users/42",
                        "POST /users { \"name\": \"Ava\" }",
                        "PUT /users/42",
                        "PATCH /users/42",
                        "DELETE /users/42"
                    ]
                }
            ],
            "quiz": [
                {
                    "question": "In the client-server model, who typically initiates an HTTP request?",
                    "choices": [
                        "The client",
                        "The server",
                        "The database",
                        "The DNS"
                    ],
                    "answer": 0
                },
                {
                    "question": "Which HTTP method is typically used to retrieve data?",
                    "choices": [
                        "POST",
                        "GET",
                        "PUT",
                        "DELETE"
                    ],
                    "answer": 1
                },
                {
                    "question": "Which HTTP method is typically used to create a new resource?",
                    "choices": [
                        "GET",
                        "DELETE",
                        "POST",
                        "PATCH"
                    ],
                    "answer": 2
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "building-rest-apis",
            "title": "Building REST APIs",
            "pages": [
                {
                    "content": "A REST API exposes resources, such as users or orders, through endpoints. A resource is an entity or piece of data the API exposes. An endpoint is a URL through which the API exposes a resource or operation.",
                    "examples": [
                        "/api/users",
                        "/api/orders",
                        "/api/orders/7"
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "content": "REST emphasizes predictable, resource-based URLs. The URL names the resource, and the HTTP method says what to do with it. Avoid random URLs or URLs that do not identify resources.",
                    "examples": [
                        "GET    /api/orders",
                        "GET    /api/orders/7",
                        "POST   /api/orders",
                        "DELETE /api/orders/7"
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "content": "<code>GET /api/orders/7</code> is a request to retrieve order 7. REST APIs commonly return structured data as JSON, so different clients can consume it. They do not return formats like MP3, PNG, or DOCX.",
                    "examples": [
                        "GET /api/orders/7",
                        "{",
                        "    \"id\": 7,",
                        "    \"total\": 42.50",
                        "}"
                    ]
                }
            ],
            "quiz": [
                {
                    "question": "What does a REST API expose through its endpoints?",
                    "choices": [
                        "CSS styles",
                        "Computer hardware",
                        "Browser settings",
                        "Resources"
                    ],
                    "answer": 3
                },
                {
                    "question": "What kind of URLs does a REST API generally use?",
                    "choices": [
                        "Predictable, resource-based URLs",
                        "Random URLs",
                        "URLs that only contain numbers",
                        "URLs that cannot contain resource names"
                    ],
                    "answer": 0
                },
                {
                    "question": "What format does a REST API commonly use to return structured data?",
                    "choices": [
                        "MP3",
                        "JSON",
                        "Word documents",
                        "Video files"
                    ],
                    "answer": 1
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "auth-and-status-codes",
            "title": "Authentication & Status Codes",
            "pages": [
                {
                    "content": "Status codes describe the result of a request. 200 OK means success. 201 Created means a resource was created. 400 Bad Request means the request was invalid or badly formed.",
                    "examples": [
                        "200 OK",
                        "201 Created",
                        "400 Bad Request"
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "content": "401 Unauthorized means the request lacks valid authentication. 404 Not Found means the requested resource was not found. 500 Internal Server Error means the server hit an error.",
                    "examples": [
                        "401 Unauthorized",
                        "404 Not Found",
                        "500 Internal Server Error"
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "content": "Authentication confirms who is making a request. A common approach is an authentication token sent with each request. The server checks the token before sharing protected data.",
                    "examples": [
                        "GET /api/profile",
                        "Authorization: Bearer abc123"
                    ]
                }
            ],
            "quiz": [
                {
                    "question": "What does HTTP status code 200 usually indicate?",
                    "choices": [
                        "Not found",
                        "Unauthorized",
                        "OK or successful request",
                        "Server error"
                    ],
                    "answer": 2
                },
                {
                    "question": "What does HTTP status code 404 mean?",
                    "choices": [
                        "Success",
                        "Created",
                        "Unauthorized",
                        "Not found"
                    ],
                    "answer": 3
                },
                {
                    "question": "What is authentication used for in an API?",
                    "choices": [
                        "To confirm who is making a request",
                        "To change the URL",
                        "To format JSON",
                        "To create CSS styles"
                    ],
                    "answer": 0
                }
            ]
        }
    ],
